  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal.",
  "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{\u2032}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal.",
